Z||000012||Ed||02-23-2000||11:31 PM||||The four cylinders threw a rod through the block, throwing oil everywhere including the manifolds where it could ignite. The rods usually failed due to lack of oil. The main cause was lack of maintenance by the owners in not watching the oil level.  A new dipstick was provided that somehow allowed four quarts of oil to be used instead of three.   <P>The second cause of fires was leaking antifreeze. Antifreeze will ignite if heated to the point of ignition, as, again, falling onto the exhaust manifold. I got a new water pump from GM on this recall.  <P>Some other items were fixed by dealers as part of the recall.<P>One good source of recall information is a book called "Get Your Pontiac Fixed Right" where repairs, safety alerts, recalls, and other suchlike is published. My copy is dated 1989, so I don't know if it is still available.  But some of the questions appearing on this board could be answered in this book. <P>Incidentally, this was a "hot" topic in the early years and was a constant topic of conversation in the national club magazines in the days before the internet. <P>In the 15 years I've owned Fieros I've never personally met anyone whose car burned. However, my work truck, a Dodge Ram 4X caught on fire when it was improperly repaired by a shop.    <P>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 02-23-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Ed (edited 02-24-2000).]||||reg||1
